### 4. chrome.debugger (Puppeteer-in-Browser)
Per master context - use Chrome DevTools Protocol inside the extension for stealth automation.

#### [NEW] `apps/extension/lib/puppeteer-lite.ts`
```typescript
// Attach debugger to tab
export async function attachDebugger(tabId: number) {
  await chrome.debugger.attach({ tabId }, "1.3");
}

// Human-like typing using CDP
export async function humanType(tabId: number, text: string) {
  for (const char of text) {
    await chrome.debugger.sendCommand({ tabId }, "Input.dispatchKeyEvent", {
      type: "keyDown", text: char
    });
    await humanDelay(50, 150); // Random delay between keystrokes
  }
}

// Scroll with CDP
export async function scrollPage(tabId: number) {
  await chrome.debugger.sendCommand({ tabId }, "Input.synthesizeScrollGesture", {
    x: 100, y: 100, yDistance: -500
  });
}
```

**Why chrome.debugger vs traditional Puppeteer:**
- Uses YOUR real browser session (logged-in cookies)
- No headless detection
- Simulates real key events (anti-bot bypass)
- Runs inside extension, no separate Node process

---

### 5. Human Jitter Delays
```typescript
export const humanDelay = (min = 1000, max = 3000) => 
  new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, min + Math.random() * (max - min)));
```

---

